"Overlays a fitted relationship on observed paired data to show model form and departures.\n\nUse when: A stated model describes an association that readers need to evaluate visually. Observed scatter should remain visible beside a fitted trend.\n\nAvoid when: The fit would be presented without enough information to assess assumptions or data coverage.\n\nVisual grammar: Point positions encode paired observations. A line encodes fitted conditional values, with any band explicitly identified as confidence or prediction uncertainty.\n\nBuild: Plot the included observations before fitting. Fit a named model or smoother with documented parameters and weighting. Draw fitted values only over justified predictor support and identify the model.\n\nCheck: Check transformations and coefficients match the displayed axes. Verify fitting and plotted data use the same inclusion rules. Inspect residual diagnostics before presenting model adequacy.\n\nPitfalls: A smooth line does not prove causation or good out-of-sample prediction. Do not extrapolate beyond the observed predictor range without a clear justification and visible distinction.\n\nExample: Plot synthetic study time versus assessment score with a fitted linear trend and a stated confidence band.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot, Residual plot, Confidence band."
